## Context

`c2c install grok` is the supported CLI-first path. It installs the generated assembled Grok `/c2c` skill, a dynamic `c2c-session` identity skill, and SessionStart/SessionEnd hooks invoking `c2c hook grok`. It installs no MCP configuration. Preferred inbound remains a persistent Grok Monitor running `c2c monitor`.

B206 made that Monitor path truthful and usable for aliases without relay identity binding: a signed preflight disables only relay watch while local monitoring continues, and `--register-relay-alias` provides explicit bootstrap when wanted. B207's shared advisory trust guidance is included in the assembled Grok skill. These improve CLI-first delivery; they do not create a plugin package.

## Desired later

A thin Grok plugin package, once Grok's supported plugin/marketplace format is stable, that reuses rather than forks:

- the generated assembled Grok skill;
- the existing `c2c hook grok` SessionStart/SessionEnd contract;
- the dynamic session-identity skill behavior;
- an optional `/c2c` command;
- optional MCP configuration only through explicit opt-in.

The plugin must not maintain independent copies of safety guidance, CLI recipes, hook logic, or relay setup.

## Why defer

CLI + global hooks + skill already cover vanilla Grok without trust/marketplace friction.
Plugin is packaging polish + one-step trust model, not delivery quality.

Local `c2c monitor` receive must work regardless of relay registration. For cross-host relay watch, preserve B206 preflight semantics: a missing alias-to-machine-identity binding leaves relay watch off while local monitoring continues. Packaging must never silently bind an alias; it may surface explicit `c2c relay register --alias <alias>` or `c2c monitor --alias <alias> --register-relay-alias` actions.

## Depends on

Landed: skill snippet assembly, `c2c install grok`, `c2c hook grok`, `GROK_SESSION_ID`, B206 relay-watch preflight/bootstrap, and B207 shared trust guidance.

## Acceptance (when picked up)

- Plugin installation exposes the same supported Grok surfaces as `c2c install grok`, using generated/shared sources.
- Installation is idempotent and defines ownership/coexistence when `c2c install grok` was already run.
- Plugin uninstall and `c2c uninstall grok` remove only artifacts they own and leave no stale duplicate hook or skill entries.
- No MCP configuration is installed unless explicitly opted in.
- Generated-skill drift, hook behavior, Monitor-local fallback, relay-preflight behavior, and install/uninstall coexistence have conformance tests.
- Validate Grok's actual supported plugin command and marketplace layout when implementation begins; do not freeze a speculative command now.
